**Reasoning**

1. **Initial water‑vapour moles**  
   The chamber has total volume \(V_{\text{tot}}=100\;\text{L}\).  
   Initially the liquid occupies \(V_{\text{liq},i}=1\;\text{L}\), so the vapour occupies  

   \[
   V_{i}=V_{\text{tot}}-V_{\text{liq},i}=99\;\text{L}
   \]

   at temperature \(T_i=363.15\;\text{K}\) and partial pressure \(p_i=0.690\;\text{atm}\).

   Using the ideal‑gas equation  

   \[
   n_i=\frac{p_iV_i}{RT_i}
   \]

   \[
   n_i=\frac{(0.690\;\text{atm})(99\;\text{L})}
            {(0.082057\;\text{L·atm·mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1})(363.15\;\text{K})}
          \approx 2.29\;\text{mol}
   \]

2. **Additional moles that vaporised**  
   From part 5.4 (or the prescribed value) the amount of water that flashes is  

   \[
   n_{\text{vap}}=0.56\;\text{mol}
   \]

3. **Final conditions**  
   After the flash the liquid volume is \(V_{\text{liq},f}=2\;\text{L}\), so the vapour volume is  

   \[
   V_f = V_{\text{tot}}-V_{\text{liq},f}=98\;\text{L}
   \]

   The final temperature is \(T_f=370.15\;\text{K}\).

   Total moles of vapour present after flashing  

   \[
   n_{\text{tot}} = n_i + n_{\text{vap}}
                  \approx 2.29\;\text{mol} + 0.56\;\text{mol}
                  = 2.85\;\text{mol}
   \]

4. **Final vapour pressure**  

   \[
   p_f = \frac{n_{\text{tot}}RT_f}{V_f}
        =\frac{(2.85\;\text{mol})(0.082057\;\text{L·atm·mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1})(370.15\;\text{K})}
               {98\;\text{L}}
        \approx 0.88\;\text{atm}
   \]

   (Rounded to three significant figures, consistent with the data.)

