**1.  Amount of CO₂ introduced**

The gas is bubbled through the reactor at  

\[
\dot V = 3.0\ \text{L min}^{-1},\qquad 
t = 7\ \text{h}=420\ \text{min}
\]

\[
V_{\text{tot}} = \dot V\,t = 3.0\;\text{L min}^{-1}\times420\;\text{min}=1260\ \text{L}
\]

At \(T=25^{\circ}\text{C}=298\ \text{K}\) and \(P=1\ \text{atm}\),

\[
n_{\text{CO}_2}^{\text{in}}=\frac{PV}{RT}
          =\frac{1\;\text{atm}\times1260\;\text{L}}
                 {0.08206\;\text{L atm mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}\times298\;\text{K}}
          \approx 5.15\times10^{1}\ \text{mol}
\]

\[
n_{\text{CO}_2}^{\text{in}}\approx 51.5\ \text{mol}
\]

---

**2.  Moles of CO₂ that have reacted**

Only two products are reported in the 100 mL solution:

\[
\begin{aligned}
c(\text{HCHO}) &=1.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}
        \;\Rightarrow\; n_{\text{HCHO}} =0.001\;\frac{\text{mol}}{\text{L}}\times0.100\;\text{L}
                                      =1.0\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol} \\[4pt]
c(\text{CH}_3\text{OH}) &=5.4\times10^{-3}\ \text{M}
        \;\Rightarrow\; n_{\text{CH}_3\text{OH}} =0.0054\;\frac{\text{mol}}{\text{L}}\times0.100\;\text{L}
                                          =5.4\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol}
\end{aligned}
\]

Each mole of HCHO or CH₃OH originates from **one mole of CO₂**, therefore  

\[
n_{\text{CO}_2}^{\text{conv}} = n_{\text{HCHO}}+n_{\text{CH}_3\text{OH}}
                              = 1.0\times10^{-4}+5.4\times10^{-4}
                              = 6.4\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol}
\]

---

**3.  Total degree of CO₂ conversion, \(\eta\)**  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
\eta =\frac{n_{\text{CO}_2}^{\text{conv}}}{n_{\text{CO}_2}^{\text{in}}}
     =\frac{6.4\times10^{-4}}{5.15\times10^{1}}
     \approx 1.2\times10^{-5}
     }
\]

In percent: \(\eta \approx 0.0012\%\).

---

**4.  Number of incident photons, \(N_i\)**  

Lamp power \(P=500\ \text{W}=500\ \text{J s}^{-1}\).  
Irradiation time \(t=7\ \text{h}=25200\ \text{s}\).

\[
E_{\text{tot}} = Pt = 500\;\text{J s}^{-1}\times25200\;\text{s}=1.26\times10^{7}\ \text{J}
\]

Photon energy at \(\lambda =365\ \text{nm}\):

\[
E_{\text{ph}} = \frac{hc}{\lambda}
              = \frac{(6.626\times10^{-34}\ \text{J s})(3.00\times10^{8}\ \text{m s}^{-1})}
                     {365\times10^{-9}\ \text{m}}
              \approx 5.45\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

\[
N_i = \frac{E_{\text{tot}}}{E_{\text{ph}}}
    = \frac{1.26\times10^{7}\ \text{J}}{5.45\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}}
    \approx 2.3\times10^{25}\ \text{photons}
\]

---

**5.  Number of electrons transferred, \(N_e\)**  

Redox equations (in acidic solution):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{CO}_2 + 4\;\text{H}^{+}+4e^{-} &\rightarrow \text{HCHO}+ \text{H}_2\text{O} \\
\text{CO}_2 + 6\;\text{H}^{+}+6e^{-} &\rightarrow \text{CH}_3\text{OH}+ \text{H}_2\text{O}
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence the electron consumption per mole of product is 4 e⁻ for HCHO and 6 e⁻ for CH₃OH.

\[
\begin{aligned}
n_{e^-}&=4\,n_{\text{HCHO}}+6\,n_{\text{CH}_3\text{OH}}   \\
       &=4(1.0\times10^{-4})+6(5.4\times10^{-4})            \\
       &=4.0\times10^{-4}+3.24\times10^{-3}
        =3.64\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol e}^{-}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
N_e = n_{e^-}\,N_A
    =3.64\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\times6.022\times10^{23}\ \text{mol}^{-1}
    \approx 2.2\times10^{21}\ \text{electrons}
\]

---

**6.  Apparent quantum yield, \(\varphi\)**  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
\varphi = \frac{N_e}{N_i}
        = \frac{2.2\times10^{21}}{2.3\times10^{25}}
        \approx 9.5\times10^{-5}
        }
\]

In percent: \(\varphi \approx 0.0095\%\).

---

